X-Google-Attributes: gidf678f,public
From: patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii)
Subject: Re: how to do low level diskcopy?
Date: 1999/04/06
Message-ID: <7edrqq$ca0@ds2.acs.ucalgary.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 463389611
References: <KfsO2.36$op.172130816@news.telia.no>
Organization: The University of Calgary
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.misc

Erling Kopperdal (erling.kopperdal@unique.no) wrote:
: Someone gave me an old DEC3000, M300 machine for free. It's running Digital
: Unix V3.2 (prev: DEC OSF/1).  I have two SCSI disks (1.05GB).  Before I
: start to play with it, I want to copy the OS-disk to the other disk in order
: to have a backup (I don't need the data on this machine).  I tried to use
: the dd command (dd if=/dev/rrz0c of=/dev/rrz2c), but then it complains about
: 'read only' filesystem.

Skip block 0 on the output partition: it contains the volume label and is
not writeable through the regular disk devices. Something like:

dd if=/dev/rrz0c of=/dev/rrz2c bs=512 iseek=1 oseek=1 count=65535
dd if=/dev/rrz0c of=/dev/rrz2c bs=64k iseek=1 oseek=1

should work fine.

Regards,

/Serge.P



